// Clustering-time context predicates. Predicates take only the FunctionExpression — no
// `vars`, no `state` — so they are safe to evaluate at clustering time against the stub
// `defaultState` that the relational StoreContract passes in. The narrower function type
// makes this invariant statically enforced: a state-reading predicate cannot be placed
// here because State is not in scope.
//
// Two flavours of entry are registered:
//   (a) real AST-shape predicates (e.g. `canProcessAt`, `canProcessMatch`,
//       `canProcessTypeName`, `isNewPairFunction`) — the relational cluster claims the FE
//       only when the predicate fires true, allowing the FE to fall through to the
//       platform (or another store's) cluster when it fires false. Example: `->at(0)` on
//       an already-routed non-`NoSet` `RoutedValueSpecification` correctly defers to
//       the platform cluster (see test `testPlatformExpressionDependencyOnAFromExpression`).
//   (b) unconditional-true predicates `{fe | true}` for context-only functions whose
//       sole handler lives in getContextBasedSupportedFunctions and would otherwise not
//       be visible to the cluster-membership check. Today the always-claim entries are
//       `fold`, `isNotEmpty_Any_MANY`, `indexOf`, `instanceOf`. A mis-claim then falls
//       through to `processContextOnlyFunctionFallback` at PureToSQL time and fails
//       loudly with a diagnostic message.
